em when I talk to Silicon Valley  utopians I kind of get this idea that  let's hate late that what they kind of  say is let's hasten the day when there's  no more work because then it will be all  Burning Man all the time and I just I  don't see that in the communities where  work is actually going away over and  over again so I was trying to articulate  that this is an actual problem that we  need to worry about in this community  needs to worry about  I completely agree with Andrew my  number-one concern for this weekend is  what's been happening with the  intersection of Technology and jobs and  wages so I was trying to crystallize  that and the best I could come up with  earlier this year is something I'm  calling maccabe's passion and it's that  if current trends continue the people  are going to rise up before the machines  do this caution has two really powerful  virtues number one is it fits in a tweet  number two is that it's got a fantastic  visual aid so that's our incoming  president on the left and that's Nigel  Faraj the leader of the brexit movement  in the United Kingdom Moshe I agree  we're already seeing symbols of  discontent that are altering our  political landscape are going to alter  our society for at least the next four  years so second to last question well  okay are things going to get any better  is there any reason to believe that  these trends are going to reverse and  that we're heading into a happier future  I take a lot of heart from the kind of  optimism that Daniela puts out there and  Marc Andreessen articulates when we talk  to him and their kind of thing look this  is there are some problems with this  period we're living through but calm  down the lot of people have been talking  about mass technological unemployment  all these horrible things that come with  automation for 200 years for 200 years  the economy has grown the workforce has  grown average incomes has grown things  have gotten massively better I  completely agree with Jeff for a lot and  a lot of people we are under estimating  that engine of prosperity and meaning  out there history is going 200 years of  industrial history are going to repeat  themselves the patterns going to  continue there's another view that says  no the thing that we're under estimating  actually is technological progress and  especially this burst that we're all  excited about and that a lot of us are  contributing to the power of modern  technology is putting us into a  different regime this time actually is  different and the trends that that we've  seen here are are an early indicator of  this different period that we're  actually in and if you believe this the  natural conclusion is that some of the  trends these trends are going to  continue and some of them are probably  going to accelerate personally I believe  that so finally what should we do about  this and I'm gonna follow Jeff's lead  and actually make stuff up out of whole  cloth and what I'm gonna make up as a  Venn diagram that tries to categorize  the solutions that you hear based on the  audience that you're interacting with so  the three circles on my Venn diagram are  economists technologists and politicians  and I want to fill this in a completely  idiosyncratic subjective and non  scientific way a few politicians like  trade wars nobody else likes trade wars  I think Trump found the one economist  who has a PhD and teaches in a  university in the country who thinks a  trade war might be a healthy thing for  the American economy nobody else really  believes that a couple other things that  politicians are fond of where you find a  bit more commonality with some other  constituents Jeff did a very nice job of  articulating an economists view of  lowering income tax on people and the  idea that bounty will happen as a result  of that some politicians like a minimum  wage a couple economists do but in  general Akana  don't like artificial ceilings or floors  on prices they seem they tend to think  that perverts the proper working of  things there is some commonality between  what economists like Jeff mentioned a  wage subsidy for lower income workers we  have one of those in the United States  it's called the Earned Income Tax Credit  both Paul Ryan and Obama have said it  should be expanded most of the  economists that we talk to you agree  pretty strongly with that  in contrast a lot of technologists that  I talk to are I believe overexcited  about this notion of a basic income this  idea we are a wealthy society we can  afford to distribute cash to people  let's do that I agree that we should be  helping people earn more money but when  I look at the graphs that Murray put  together about the social woes I  honestly struggle to think how any of  them would be fixed by dropping money  out of helicopters in these communities  it just absolutely does not compute to  me I like work because of the dignity  and the value in the community that come  along for him work in our last book Eric  and I put a quote from Voltaire him that  I continue to fall back on he said work  saves us from three great evils boredom  vice and need of those three the  universal basic income only takes care  of nee need I think it's by far the  least of the three evils so where is  there some commonality the economists  and technologists that we hang around  with our adamant about the need to  liberalize immigration the fact that  we're keeping out some of the most  talented and tenacious people in the  world who want to come here to build  their lives in careers it's just it's  Kafkaesque it's nonsense and then the  idea that we have a couple large  companies that are giving working people  without advanced skills the opportunity  to learn live earn a living by driving a  car or by opening up rooms in their  homes to rent out to people and we're  putting all kinds of roadblocks in their  way I find that ludicrous if we're  interested in helping people earn a  living we should be interested in  companies that help people actually earn  a living and then finally if there  anything in that happy intersection I'm  being a little bit optimistic about this  I think there are a couple things  Trump has talked about infrastructure  we'll see what happens there but you  have to go a long long way before you  find an economist or a technologist that  I've talked to who doesn't want  better physical and digital  infrastructure and then finally we've  heard over and over and over about the  importance of educational reform I agree  with that I think it's probably  necessary I do not believe for a second  that it's sufficient so I apologize for  that quick drink from a fire hose but I  think evidence is always helpful in  these discussions Thanks  [Music]
 

